@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using SpaceSails.Core
@*
    #251 · THE FLOW COLUMN, ON ITS OWN FILE — the last nested layout the page still owned.

    #1107 made every block of Map.razor a surface; #1172 gathered the runs of gates that were left into
    eleven racks. What neither could take is what is here: not a card and not a run of cards, but the page's
    one piece of LAYOUT — the window-tall box #992 built so the Plotting panel's cap could be measured
    instead of guessed, with the masthead at its head (#195), the desks in its greedy middle (#236), and the
    story plate (#994) and the navigation-target panel (#1037) taking their own measured heights off its
    foot. Five children, and ninety lines of prose saying why each one stands where it stands.

    That prose is about the COLUMN, so it lives in the column's own file now, and Map.razor is left a flat
    list of invocations with no nesting in it at all.

    The head of the column is a BOX rather than a single gated surface — a div with the desk tabs and the
    pilot banner in it — so it is `TopStackBox.razor` beside this file and the column invokes it. The other
    four children are one existing surface behind one gate each; a wrapper around any of those would be a
    second copy of that surface's parameter list and no new boundary, so they stand here.

    Moved by PURE MOTION: the gates came with the invocations, the comments came with the boxes they
    explain, and every page member the block reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME — the fields keep
    their underscore, the methods come across as delegates. Not one character between the fences changed,
    which is what lets MapMarkup splice it back and the composed page stay byte-identical.

    EIGHT members the moved markup ASSIGNS cross as PAIRS (the value in, the page's own setter out, behind
    a private property that keeps the field's own name), because the column is where they meet: the Nav
    HUD's `_burnAngleAbsolute` and `_scrubOffsetSeconds`, the sensors desk's captured `_trackingPost`, and
    the five the desks write — `_captainTab`, `_commsSelectedId`, `_credits`, `_localSpace`, `_renameDraft`.
    Two of the eight are ALSO read one-way further down the column (`_credits` by the Nav HUD,
    `_trackingPost` by the War Room); they read the private property, so both ends see one value.

    IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs). With the suppression on both sides the four
    lambdas that came in with the markup — the zoom step, the news feed, the bank, the news event — dispatch
    exactly what they dispatched from the page, which is nothing.

    The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules; it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope
    (pinned in SpaceSails.Client.csproj), so `.map-flowcolumn` and its peek-passthrough rule go on matching
    the column exactly as they matched it in the page.
*@
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    @* #195 (owner ruling: the NOW/NEXT banner covered the toolbar's 'checklist' and '?' buttons):
       the desk tabs, the pilot banner and the ship-alert strip live in ONE in-FLOW column now (was three
       independently top-anchored absolute layers whose fixed `top`s assumed a one-row banner). Because
       they claim real document-flow height, and the Nav toolbar/readout HUD (.map-hud) below is in flow
       too, a 2–3 row banner PUSHES the toolbar down instead of floating over it — a status surface can
       never again hide a control (#123). *@
    @* #992 · THE FLOW COLUMN IS BOUND TO THE WINDOW. Owner, docked at The Red Eye with a four-step plan
       and the last step's editor open: the Plotting panel ran off the bottom of the window and the ±p /
       ±d / ±h rows could not be reached at all. #950's cap on .map-plot was `100vh - 14rem` — a CONSTANT
       guess at how much chrome stands above the panel — and the Nav HUD puts far more than 14rem there
       (the tab bar and the banner, then the toolbar, the readouts block and the frame chip), so the cap
       sat some 300 px below the fold and never once engaged.

       This is that same fact MEASURED instead of guessed. .map-topstack and .map-hud are the only two
       in-flow children of the map page (#195 put them there), so wrapping the pair in one column that is
       exactly the window tall lets flex do the arithmetic: the top stack claims whatever height its
       banner really needs, the HUD gets the rest, and the Plotting panel inside it can only ever be as
       tall as what is left. A four-row banner tomorrow moves the cap by itself.

       Deliberately UNPOSITIONED (no `position`, no `z-index`): the two children keep their own positioned
       boxes in .map-page's stacking context exactly as before, so nothing that paints over the HUD (cards,
       backdrops, the scope) changes layer. pointer-events:none because this box covers the whole window
       and the canvas has to stay grabbable through it — the same bargain the two children already make,
       each re-enabling its own controls. *@
    @* #1038 · peek-passthrough on BOTH boxes: neither is an overlay, they are the layout that #992 put
       between the map page and the desk tab bar, and the peek fade has to walk THROUGH them to reach the
       panels rather than dropping the whole column (and the 👁 way out inside it) to opacity 0. See the
       .map-peek rule in app.css — the classes are the contract, the nesting is not. *@
