    @* #236 · THE DESKS JOIN THE FLOW COLUMN — the whole of this lane, in one move.

       A full-screen desk used to be `position: absolute; inset: 0` with `padding-top:
       --desk-top-clearance`, a hand-tuned 8.25rem that stood for "how tall can the masthead above me
       get?". It was tuned twice (5.75rem for the one-row banner, then 8.25rem when the #190 voice grew
       the banner to three rows and slid every desk's own first row underneath it) and it was a guess
       both times — the owner's rule (#236, 2026-07-17) is that the banner's real estate is a no-button
       zone on EVERY screen, and a guess cannot keep that promise.

       So the desks stop guessing and start FLOWING. .map-flowcolumn is already the window-tall column
       #992 built for the top stack and the Nav HUD; a desk is the same kind of thing — a box that must
       begin exactly where the masthead ends — so it becomes the column's next child instead of a
       stranger anchored to the page with a number. A three-row banner with an alert strip under it now
       PUSHES the desk down by construction; there is nothing left to keep in sync, because there is no
       longer a second place the height is written.

       Never mind the pointer-events note below: .desk-layer already declares its own `pointer-events:
       auto` (it is the desk's shield), so it re-enables its clicks inside the column exactly as
       .map-topstack and .map-hud do. And the scrim it paints over the whole window is unchanged —
       .desk-layer::before carries it up over the band, so the masthead still stands on the desk's own
       dark ground rather than on the live map (DeskPanels.razor.css).

       WHY HERE AND NOT AT THE BOTTOM OF THE COLUMN. A desk stands where .map-hud stands — the greedy
       middle, `flex: 1 1 auto` — and the story plate below it has to stay the column's LAST item, which
       is the whole of #994 item 2: the plate lands on the pixels it always landed on precisely because
       the flow hands it its own height at the FOOT. The first cut of this move put the desks after it
       and the plate was squeezed to the top of the column instead — measured at 1280×720, the Captain
       desk's own first row slid from y 140 to y 168 and the plate's ✕ appeared at y 120, in the band. *@

